  "final_text": "I created both the dashboard and the alert rule, and the rule is enabled and running. Its status is `ok`, which means it has run and is not firing right now.\n\n**1. What the data holds.** `kibana_sample_data_logs` is web server access logs, with `timestamp` as the time field. Each record has the client IP, the requested URL, the response size in bytes, the source and destination country, the host, the OS, the user agent and the response code. There are about 14,000 requests from 4 Oct to 3 Dec 2026, and only three response codes appear:\n\n| Response code | Requests |\n|---|---|\n| 200 | 12,832 |\n| 404 | 801 |\n| 503 | 441 |\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-1bd912fb Web logs overview\".** It has 7 panels and is at http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-1bd912fb-web-logs-overview-9b62f374485c\n- Single numbers for total requests, unique visitors (counted by client IP) and total bytes served.\n- Requests over time, split by response code.\n- A pie chart of the share of each response code.\n- A table of the top 10 URLs, with request count and average bytes.\n- The top 10 source countries.\n\nIts default time range runs from 7 days ago to 60 days ahead, so it covers the sample data, which extends into December.\n\n**3. Alert rule \"EFF-1bd912fb 5xx spike\"** (id `5cad16a7-a743-48a3-811f-d48300f78429`). It is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs` that checks every minute. It fires when more than 10 matching requests arrive in the last 5 minutes. A request matches when its response code starts with \"5\", so the rule would catch any 5xx code, not just the 503s in this data.\n\nThe rule has no action attached, so when it fires the alert only shows up in Kibana and nobody is notified. If you want notifications, a connector such as Slack or email needs to be added.",
